{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011Cfbn5EoZXPQHwHLvQ4f57", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "This pattern matches `YYYY-MM-DD` and accepts only months 01\u201312 and days 01\u201331:\n\n```regex\n^\\d{4}-(0[1-9]|1[0-2])-(0[1-9]|[12]\\d|3[01])$\n```\n\n- `\\d{4}`: a four-digit year\n- `(0[1-9]|1[0-2])`: a month from 01 to 12\n- `(0[1-9]|[12]\\d|3[01])`: a day from 01 to 31\n- `^\u2026$`: the whole string must be the date. Drop these, or use `\\b\u2026\\b`, to find dates inside longer text.\n\nIt matches `2026-10-01` and rejects `2026-13-01`, `2026-10-32` and `26-10-01`.\n\n**Limitation:** the regex doesn't check how many days a month has, so it accepts dates like `2026-02-30`. If you need to know the date is real, use the regex to check the format and then parse the date. For example, in Python use `datetime.date.fromisoformat(s)`; in JavaScript, parse it and check that the result still formats back to the same string.\n\nIf you only need the shape, `^\\d{4}-\\d{2}-\\d{2}$` is enough."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 287, "cache_read_input_tokens": 23992, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 287}, "output_tokens": 394, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "not_available"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "2fb6220d-d432-4d3f-a310-634f414cb498", "uuid": "ac3802ea-b2ab-431e-b82c-e5806ae7026f", "timestamp": "2026-10-01T15:46:45.575Z", "request_id": "req_011Cfbn5ETEuBTYQKC1oLaGA"}
